>HTTP MODULE CREATING OWN Server, HEADERS MANAGEMENT

Creating your own server using the HTTP module in Node.js is a great way to learn about server-side development. The HTTP module allows you to create a server, manage requests and responses, and handle HTTP headers. Let's go through the steps to create a simple HTTP server and manage headers.

Step-by-Step Guide
    1. Creating a Simple HTTP Server
    server.js
    ---------------------------------
    const http = require('http');

    // Create an HTTP server
    const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
        // Set the response HTTP headers with a status code and content type
        res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
        
        // Send the response body
        res.end('Hello, World!\n');
    });

    // The server listens on port 3000
    server.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1', () => {
        console.log('Server running at http://127.0.0.1:3000/');
    });

    ---------------------------------

    Explanation
    ->Importing the HTTP Module:

    const http = require('http'); - This imports the HTTP module.

    ->Creating the Server:

    http.createServer((req, res) => { ... }); - This creates an HTTP server that listens for requests.

    The callback function takes req (request) and res (response) objects as parameters.

    ->Setting HTTP Headers:

    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' }); - This sets the status code to 200 (OK) and the content type to text/plain.

    ->Sending the Response:

    res.end('Hello, World!\n'); - This sends the response body and ends the response.

    ->Listening on a Port:

    server.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1', () => { ... }); - This starts the server and listens on port 3000. The callback function runs when the server starts.

    2. Handling Different Routes and Headers
    server.js

    ------------------------------------
    const http = require('http');

    const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
        if (req.url === '/') {
            res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' });
            res.end('<h1>Welcome to the Home Page!</h1>');
        } else if (req.url === '/about') {
            res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' });
            res.end('<h1>About Us</h1><p>This is the about page.</p>');
        } else {
            res.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' });
            res.end('<h1>404 Not Found</h1>');
        }
    });

    server.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1', () => {
        console.log('Server running at http://127.0.0.1:3000/');
    });

    ------------------------------------

    Explanation
    ->Handling Routes:

    ->The server checks req.url to determine the requested URL path.

    ->For the home page (/), it responds with an HTML welcome message.

    ->For the about page (/about), it responds with an HTML about message.

    ->For any other path, it responds with a 404 Not Found error.

=>Setting Different Headers:

    Depending on the route, different headers are set using res.writeHead().

    3. Managing Custom Headers
    server.js
    -------------------------------------
    const http = require('http');

    const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
        // Set custom headers
        res.setHeader('Custom-Header', 'This is a custom header');
        
        if (req.url === '/') {
            res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' });
            res.end('<h1>Welcome to the Home Page!</h1>');
        } else if (req.url === '/json') {
            res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
            res.end(JSON.stringify({ message: 'Hello, JSON!' }));
        } else {
            res.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' });
            res.end('<h1>404 Not Found</h1>');
        }
    });

    server.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1', () => {
        console.log('Server running at http://127.0.0.1:3000/');
    });

    -------------------------------------
    Explanation
    ->Setting Custom Headers:

    res.setHeader('Custom-Header', 'This is a custom header'); - This sets a custom header for the response.

    ->Handling JSON Response:

    The server can also handle JSON responses by setting the content type to application/json and using JSON.stringify() to convert an object to a JSON string.

    Summary
    By following these steps, you can create a simple HTTP server in Node.js, manage different routes, and handle custom HTTP headers. This foundational knowledge is essential for building more complex web applications with Node.js.